

CONVEX — Asset Allocation Philosophy

CONVEX does not allocate capital based on forecasts, narratives, or static diversification rules.
Their philosophy is built around risk geometry, regime awareness, and asymmetry.

At its core:

Capital must be positioned where the payoff is convex and withdrawn where losses are concave.

⸻

1. Core Principle: Convexity over Prediction

CONVEX explicitly rejects:
	•	Point forecasts (price targets, GDP guesses)
	•	Linear risk models (mean–variance, VaR-only)
	•	Buy-and-hold regardless of regime

Instead, allocation is driven by:
	•	Fragility vs. antifragility
	•	Distribution shape (fat tails vs thin tails)
	•	Asymmetric payoff structures

They care less about what will happen
and more about what happens if something extreme occurs.

⸻

2. Fragility Is the Allocation Trigger

Every asset class is evaluated through the same lens:

State	Meaning	Allocation Action
Convex / Antifragile	Small improvements → large upside	Increase exposure
Neutral	Balanced risk/reward	Maintain
Concave / Fragile	Small shocks → large losses	Reduce or avoid

This applies equally to:
	•	Equities (VISA AÇÕES)
	•	Crypto (CFM)
	•	FX (USD/BRL)
	•	Bonds
	•	Commodities

Allocation changes when fragility changes, not when headlines change.

⸻

3. Regime-Aware, Not Static Allocation

CONVEX portfolios are regime-contingent, not strategic-static.

They assume markets move through:
	•	Liquidity contraction → fragility
	•	Liquidity inflection → convexity
	•	Excess → late-cycle instability

Therefore:
	•	Asset weights are dynamic
	•	“Cash” and “optionality” are legitimate allocations
	•	Inactivity is a position, not a failure

This is why long periods of no action are considered correct behavior.

⸻

4. Capital Preservation Comes First

A foundational belief:

You cannot benefit from convexity if you are wiped out before it arrives.

Hence:
	•	Avoiding drawdowns is more important than maximizing CAGR
	•	Exposure is scaled gradually
	•	Leverage is treated as fragility, not efficiency
	•	Rebalancing is slow, disciplined, and rule-based

This is Taleb/Niederhoffer logic applied to real portfolios.

⸻

5. Allocation by “Gavetas” (Buckets)

CONVEX structures portfolios into functional buckets, not asset labels.

Examples:
	•	Growth convexity (equities, crypto)
	•	Reserve of value (gold, bitcoin)
	•	Optionality / defense (cash, stablecoins)
	•	FX exposure (USD vs local currencies)

Each bucket:
	•	Has a target range, not a fixed weight
	•	Expands or contracts based on system fragility
	•	Is managed independently to avoid correlation traps

⸻

6. Liquidity Is the Master Variable

Across all reports, one variable dominates decisions:

Global liquidity

They monitor:
	•	Monetary aggregates
	•	Funding rates
	•	Market-maker behavior
	•	Forced selling vs voluntary flows

Key belief:

Price is secondary. Liquidity determines survivability and upside.

This explains why CONVEX often positions before sentiment turns.

⸻

7. Anti-Narrative, Pro-Structure

CONVEX explicitly distinguishes:
	•	Signal vs noise
	•	Structural flows vs emotional flows
	•	Forced behavior vs discretionary behavior

Retail sentiment is largely ignored.
Institutional constraints and mechanics are prioritized.

⸻

8. What CONVEX Is Not

To be explicit, CONVEX is not:
	•	Tactical trading
	•	Momentum chasing
	•	Relative value optimization
	•	Benchmark hugging
	•	Macro storytelling

It is a risk-engineering framework.

⸻

One-Sentence Summary

CONVEX allocates capital based on where losses are limited and gains are nonlinear, adjusting exposure only when the system’s fragility changes — not when opinions change.

